  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date the federal government obligated $6B to Service-Disabled Veteran-Owned Small Business set-asides. Those obligations came in 12,310 awards with an average award size of $489K. Obligations fell 30.7% year over year, and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). Yearly context shows FY21 obligations of $7.2B, FY22 obligations of $8B, FY23 obligations of $9.1B, FY24 obligations of $8.8B, and FY25 obligations of $8.7B, before the current FY26 total of $6B. The largest buyer so far is the Department of Veterans Affairs with $4.8B in obligations, followed by the Department of Defense with $600M and the Department of Homeland Security with $115M. Construction and IT are the biggest industries by dollars, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.6B and Other Computer Related Services at $1.3B. Top vendors include V3GATE, LLC at $346M and FOUR POINTS TECHNOLOGY, L.L.C. at $252M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 to date compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 30.7% year over year, with total obligations at $6B in FY2026 to date compared with $8.7B in FY25. The drop follows higher totals in FY23 of $9.1B and FY24 of $8.8B. Award volume remained sizable at 12,310 awards, producing an average award size of $489K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where are the dollars concentrated and who wins the most?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs accounts for the largest share with $4.8B in obligations, well above the Department of Defense at $600M. Construction and IT industries lead the sector mix, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.6B and Other Computer Related Services at $1.3B. The largest vendors are V3GATE, LLC at $346M and FOUR POINTS TECHNOLOGY, L.L.C. at $252M."
      }
    ]
  },
